/-!
# `Submission/Work/MotWire` — the characteristic-`5` input of the wire

This area wires the six holes of `Submission.lean`
(`theorem_1_2_1_a`, `theorem_1_2_1_b`, `theorem_1_2_1_c`, `theorem_1_2_1_d`, `theorem_1_2_2`,
`theorem_1_2_3`) to the results delivered by the other subprojects of this workspace, leaving (at
most) **one** named input: the characteristic-`5` statement below.

## Why a characteristic-`5` input is needed at all

* `theorem_1_2_2` (subfields of `ℂ`) and the characteristic-`≠ 5` half of `theorem_1_2_3` are
  delivered outright (`Work.MotV.theorem_1_2_2'`).
* `theorem_1_2_1 (a)` (number fields) and `theorem_1_2_1 (b)` (function fields over number fields)
  are in characteristic `0`, so they too are delivered outright: the T-route
  (`Work.MotT.not_gen_three_of_val`, `…_of_place`, `…_of_numberField`, `…_functionField_charNeFive`)
  applies, and its last input, audit node *T8* (`Work.MotT.LinkCertTInput`, the Crauder–Katz
  census), is discharged by the delivered `Work.MotK.LinkCertTGenInf`
  (`Submission/Work/MotWire/linkCertTInput_of_charNeFive.lean`).
* `theorem_1_2_1 (c)`, `(d)` and `theorem_1_2_3` *split* on `ringChar = 5`: in characteristic
  `≠ 5` they again follow from the T-route and from `Work.MotV.not_gen_charNeFive`, but in
  characteristic `5` nothing in this workspace yet produces a non-vanishing Lin–Shinder
  coefficient: the T-route degenerates (`μ₅` is infinitesimal, so there is no Kummer coordinate),
  and the Artin–Schreier branch `Work.MotV5` — which is designed for `K = RatFunc 𝕜` and therefore
  yields `¬ GenByPseudoRegularizable 𝕜 n` only for `n ≥ 4` (the spreading of the `K`-level
  certificate over `𝔸¹_t`, `Work.MotV.certN_of_linkCert`) — says nothing at `n = 3`, which is what
  `theorem_1_2_1 (c)`, `(d)` need in characteristic `5`.

## The input

`CharFiveInput` below is the single named characteristic-`5` input of the whole wire:

  for every *infinite* field `𝕜` of characteristic `5` and every `n ≥ 3`, `Crₙ(𝕜)` is not
  generated by pseudo-regularizable elements.

It covers the two characteristic-`5` uses:

* `theorem_1_2_1 (c)`, `(d)`: `𝕜 := X.functionField` (infinite, of characteristic `5`) and `n = 3`;
* `theorem_1_2_3`: `n ≥ 5 ≥ 3`.

`CharFiveInput` is deliberately stated in the *plain challenge vocabulary*, so that it can be
discharged later by whichever of the two characteristic-`5` routes matures
(`Submission/Work/MotV5` for `n ≥ 4` — `Submission/Work/MotWire/MotV5Input.lean` reduces the
`n ≥ 4` half of the present input to exactly the certificate hypothesis of
`Work.MotV5.not_gen_charFive_all_of_linkCert` — and the `n = 3` case, which is the paper's
`thm:ell-curves` in characteristic `5`).

## What this module provides

* `CharFiveInput`, `CharFiveInputThree` and `CharFiveInputN4` — the single input and its two
  halves (`n = 3`, resp. `n ≥ 4`);
* `charFiveInput_of_parts` — the single input from the two halves;
* `charFiveInput_three_of`, `charFiveInput_n4_of` — the two projections (the consumers use these).

No declaration of this module depends on anything but `Mathlib` and `ChallengeDeps`.
-/

namespace Work.MotWire

open AlgebraicGeometry
open MotivicInvariants (GenByPseudoRegularizable Variety)

/-- **The single named characteristic-`5` input of the wire.**  For every infinite field `𝕜` of
characteristic `5` and every `n ≥ 3`, `Crₙ(𝕜)` is not generated by pseudo-regularizable elements.

See the module docstring for why exactly this statement (and not a weaker one) is what the six
holes of `Submission.lean` consume: `theorem_1_2_1 (c)`, `(d)` need it at `n = 3` for
`𝕜 = X.functionField` with `𝔽` of characteristic `5`, and `theorem_1_2_3` needs it for `n ≥ 5`. -/
def CharFiveInput : Prop :=
  ∀ (𝕜 : Type) [Field 𝕜] [Infinite 𝕜], ringChar 𝕜 = 5 → ∀ {n : ℕ}, 3 ≤ n →
    ¬ GenByPseudoRegularizable 𝕜 n

/-- The `n = 3` half of `CharFiveInput`: the case actually consumed by `theorem_1_2_1 (c)`, `(d)`
in characteristic `5`.  (Equivalently, this is Lin–Shinder's `thm:ell-curves` in characteristic
`5`, whose geometric link is not yet constructed in this workspace.) -/
def CharFiveInputThree : Prop :=
  ∀ (𝕜 : Type) [Field 𝕜] [Infinite 𝕜], ringChar 𝕜 = 5 →
    ¬ GenByPseudoRegularizable 𝕜 3

/-- The `n ≥ 4` half of `CharFiveInput` (the characteristic-`5` branch of Theorem 1.2 (3)); by
`Work.MotWire.charFiveInputN4_of_motV5` (`Submission/Work/MotWire/MotV5Input.lean`) it follows from
the certificate hypothesis of the delivered `Work.MotV5.not_gen_charFive_all_of_linkCert`, i.e. this
half is *not* new content. -/
def CharFiveInputN4 : Prop :=
  ∀ (𝕜 : Type) [Field 𝕜] [Infinite 𝕜], ringChar 𝕜 = 5 → ∀ {n : ℕ}, 4 ≤ n →
    ¬ GenByPseudoRegularizable 𝕜 n

/-- The function-field instances of the `n = 3` half: this is the statement that
`theorem_1_2_1 (c)`, `(d)` consume in characteristic `5` (and it is exactly Lin–Shinder,
Theorem 1.2 (1) (c), (d) restricted to characteristic `5`). -/
def CharFiveFunctionFieldInput : Prop :=
  ∀ (𝔽 : Type) [Field 𝔽] (X : AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme) [Variety 𝔽 X],
    0 < topologicalKrullDim X → ringChar X.functionField = 5 →
      ¬ GenByPseudoRegularizable X.functionField 3

/-- **The single input from its two halves.** -/
theorem charFiveInput_of_parts (h3 : CharFiveInputThree) (h4 : CharFiveInputN4) :
    CharFiveInput :=
  fun 𝕜 _ _ hk {n} hn => by
    rcases eq_or_lt_of_le hn with h | h
    · rw [← h]; exact h3 𝕜 hk
    · exact h4 𝕜 hk (by omega)
/-- The `n = 3` half of the single input. -/
theorem charFiveInput_three_of (h : CharFiveInput) : CharFiveInputThree :=
  fun 𝕜 _ _ hk => h 𝕜 hk (le_refl 3)

/-- The `n ≥ 4` half of the single input. -/
theorem charFiveInput_n4_of (h : CharFiveInput) : CharFiveInputN4 :=
  fun 𝕜 _ _ hk {n} hn => h 𝕜 hk (by omega)

/-- **The single input from its function-field `n = 3` instances and the `n ≥ 4` half.**  This is
the weakest form in which the six holes consume the characteristic-`5` input; it is *weaker* than
`CharFiveInput`, whose `n = 3` half is stated for all infinite fields of characteristic `5` rather
than only for function fields of positive-dimensional varieties.

The passage from the general `CharFiveInput` is
`Work.MotWire.charFiveMinimal_of_charFiveInput` (`Submission/Work/MotWire/Minimal.lean`); it needs
the infinitude of the function field (`Work.MotWire.infinite_functionField`) and therefore lives
there rather than here. -/
def CharFiveMinimal : Prop :=
  CharFiveFunctionFieldInput ∧ CharFiveInputN4

end Work.MotWire
